<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}" class="h-full">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ settings.meta_theme_color }}">
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">

    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32, height: 32 }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    <title>
      {{- page_title -}}
      {%- if current_tags %} &middot; {{ 'general.meta.tags' | t: tags: current_tags | join: ', ' }}{% endif -%}
      {%- if current_page != 1 %} &middot; {{ 'general.meta.page' | t: page: current_page }}{% endif -%}
      {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} &middot; {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
    </title>

    {%- if page_description -%}
      <meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- render 'sb-meta-tags' -%}

    {{ 'sb-theme.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {%- render 'sb-css-variables' -%}

    {% comment %}
      TYPEFACE — Helvetica Neue is a licensed Linotype face, so there is nothing
      to fetch from a font service and Shopify's font_picker cannot offer it.
      Upload the .woff2 files to assets/ and name them in the theme settings;
      until then the stack in sb-theme.css renders Helvetica Neue natively on
      macOS/iOS and falls back to Arial elsewhere — exactly as the Next build
      does today. Nothing else changes when the files arrive.
    {% endcomment %}
    {%- if settings.font_body_woff2 != blank -%}
      <style>
        @font-face {
          font-family: 'Helvetica Neue Licensed';
          src: url('{{ settings.font_body_woff2 | asset_url }}') format('woff2');
          font-weight: 400;
          font-style: normal;
          font-display: swap;
        }
        {%- if settings.font_body_medium_woff2 != blank -%}
          @font-face {
            font-family: 'Helvetica Neue Licensed';
            src: url('{{ settings.font_body_medium_woff2 | asset_url }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 500;
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: swap;
          }
        {%- endif -%}
        :root {
          --font-sb-heading: 'Helvetica Neue Licensed';
          --font-sb-body: 'Helvetica Neue Licensed';
        }
      </style>
    {%- endif -%}

    {% comment %}
      Both scripts are deferred: nothing in the shell needs to run before paint,
      and the designs have no above-the-fold behaviour that JS provides.
    {% endcomment %}
    <script src="{{ 'sb-theme.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    <script src="{{ 'sb-nav.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    <script src="{{ 'sb-inquiry-cart.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    {%- comment -%}
      Only where there is a grid to drive. The other three are shell-wide: the
      nav and the inquiry badge are in the header on every page.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'collection' or template.name == 'search' -%}
      <script src="{{ 'sb-collection.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'product' -%}
      <script src="{{ 'sb-product.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The viewer is opened from the Gallery page and from a project, so it loads
      on pages and metaobjects — not on the catalogue, which has no lightbox.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'page' or template.name == 'metaobject' -%}
      <script src="{{ 'sb-lightbox.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The carousel is on the landing page and can be added to any page, so it
      loads on both. It does nothing when there is no track on the page.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'index' or template.name == 'page' -%}
      <script src="{{ 'sb-carousel.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
    {%- endif -%}

    {{ content_for_header }}
  </head>

  {% comment %}
    The ground. In the Next build every page composed its own PageShell and
    picked a surface; here each section carries its own `data-surface`, which is
    both more granular and editable in the theme editor. This body-level value is
    what shows between and below sections, so it is the page's base ground rather
    than a default nobody sees.

    `min-h-full flex flex-col` + `flex-1` on <main> is what keeps the footer at
    the bottom of a short page — the same three classes app/layout.tsx used.
  {% endcomment %}
  <body
    data-surface="{{ settings.page_surface }}"
    class="min-h-full flex flex-col bg-background text-foreground antialiased"
  >
    <a class="sb-skip-link" href="#main">{{ 'general.accessibility.skip_to_content' | t }}</a>

    {% sections 'header-group' %}

    <main id="main" class="flex-1" role="main">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>

    {% sections 'footer-group' %}

    {%- render 'sb-back-to-top' -%}

    <ul hidden>
      <li id="a11y-new-window-message">{{ 'general.accessibility.link_messages.new_window' | t }}</li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
